Name: markdownText

Type: property

Syntax: set the markdownText of [<chunk> of] <field> to <markdownString>

Summary:
Specifies the contents of a <field>, with its text <format|formatting>
represented as Markdown.

Associations: field

Introduced: 9.7.1

OS: mac, windows, linux, android

Platforms: desktop, server, mobile

Example:
set the markdownText of field "Notes" to URL ("file:" & tReadmePath)

Example:
put the markdownText of field "Notes" into URL ("file:" & tSavePath)

Example:
set the markdownText of line 2 of field "Notes" to "**Done**"

Value:
The <markdownText> of a field is a string.

Description:
Use the <markdownText> property to show text written in Markdown, or to
save a field's styled text as Markdown.

Setting the <markdownText> of a field (or a chunk of a field) sets its
text and the styles that Markdown can say. Each line of the Markdown is
a line of the field, and a blank line between them is left out (but
not inside a block of code between "```" or "~~~" lines, where an empty
line is an empty line of the field). It reads:
* headings, "# Heading" to "###### Heading", or text underlined with
  "===" or "---": bold, at the sizes the <HTMLText> gives h1 to h6
* "**bold**", "*italic*" and "***bold italic***" (or the same with "_")
* "`code`", and code between "```" or "~~~" lines or indented four
  spaces: in Courier, with no Markdown read inside it
* "[text](address)": the text, with its <linkText> set to the address
* "- item", "* item" and "+ item": a bulleted list, and "1. item": a
  numbered list
* "> text": a quote, indented
* "\*" and the like: the character after the backslash, as it is

A rule ("---" alone) is left out. Anything else, tables and HTML among
them, is shown as the text it is.

Getting the <markdownText> gives the text of the field (or chunk of a
field) as Markdown, with a blank line between its lines: bold, italic,
Courier, links, the heading sizes above, lists and indented lines are
written as Markdown, and other styling (colors, other fonts and sizes) is
left out, as the <RTFText> leaves out what RTF cannot say. The items of
a list follow one another with no blank line between them. Two or more
lines in a row that are all in Courier are written as one block of code,
between "```" lines, with any empty lines among them kept; one such line
on its own is written between backticks. Characters
that Markdown would read as markup are written with a backslash before
them, so a field's plain text comes back as the same text when the
<markdownText> is set to what it gave. Lines that start with four spaces
or a tab are the one exception: Markdown reads them as code.

Before the field's <markdownText> is got or set, a <getProp> or
<setProp> markdownText handler is sent to it, as for a custom property,
and the field's text is given or set only when no handler takes the
message, or one passes it. No message is sent for a chunk of a field.

>*Note:* "markdownText" is no word of the language. A field that holds
> a custom property named "markdownText" (in its default custom
> property set) keeps it: its markdownText is that custom property, as
> it always was. So is the markdownText of every object that is not a
> field, and of a field whose <customPropertySet> is not the default.
> A field that holds no such custom property gives its Markdown, where
> before it gave empty, and setting its markdownText sets its text,
> where before it made the custom property. This property is new in
> OXT-Beyond 0.2.8-dp.3, from HyperXTalk, where Emily-Elizabeth Howard
> wrote it.

References: HTMLText (property), RTFText (property),
styledText (property), text (property), linkText (property),
listStyle (property), customPropertySet (property),
getProp (control structure), setProp (control structure),
field (object)

Tags: text processing
